\begin_layout Subsection
Faster execution with the source-source compiler
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Normally when code is evaluated,
 all symbols are looked up and evaluated at runtime.
 This provides maximum flexibility for reusing code,
 as symbols like DISP or STO could have their behavior changed by storing those names as local variables.
 However,
 for improved speed,
 it's useful to reduce the number of symbol lookups and to reduce the amount of time each lookup takes.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For the latter,
 it's well to remember that the interpreter searches for local variables first;
 the closer the name is to the beginning,
 the faster it will be found.
 Conversely,
 global variables are always appended to the very end of the store,
 and thus will always be the slowest to find.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For the former,
 one way to reduce symbol lookups is to use STATIC or STATICN,
 a source-source compiler included with the language.
 They take any object and recursively delve into it,
 looking for symbols which currently resolve to a built-in or internal object.
 Where such symbols are found,
 they're replaced with a direct reference to the builtin or internal,
 thus saving a lookup each time that object is evaluated in the future.
 The compiler will return either the original object if no changes were made,
 or a new,
 compiled object.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In most situations,
 STATICN will suffice:
 it takes only one argument,
 which would typically be code but can be any type of object.
 If it's important to leave a symbol unresolved,
 STATIC additionally accepts a list of exceptions.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
While entire directories can be compiled,
 there is less chance of excitement if the directory is first removed from the store:
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
::
 Projectdir 'Projectdir RM STATICN 'Projectdir STO ;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The compiler recurses into tags,
 lists,
 code,
 quotes,
 and directories looking for symbols.
 Once a symbol is found,
 it recurses into that symbol if it exists in the named store,
 and continues to do so until finding a destination object.
 If the destination is a built-in or internal,
 the symbol is replaced.
 It will recurse to its depth limit,
 which is usually found in Static.default.depth;
 any deeper recursion is silently ignored.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
It's possible to extend the compiler to recurse into user-defined types,
 but they are safely ignored by default.
\begin_inset Newpage pagebreak
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Language reference
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Special characters
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph*
` (grave)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Any object following a 
\family typewriter
`
\family default
 will be interpreted with special preprocessing rules by the parser.
 In particular:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Comments are suppressed entirely,
 returning no object.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Symbols must exist at parse time;
 they are recalled and the recalled object is returned instead.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Composite types,
 e.g.
 quotes,
 code,
 directories,
 and lists,
 are also interpreted with the preprocessing rules applied to their contents.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph*
~ (tilde)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Any object following a 
\family typewriter
~
\family default
 will be interpreted with standard language rules.
 This can be used to force exceptions to preprocessing in a composite object.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph*

\backslash
 (backslash)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Inside symbols and strings,
 a backslash forces the next character to be treated literally.
 Within the REPL,
 a backslash at the end of a line will prompt for an additional line of input.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Special names
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph*
ARGS
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Commandline arguments issued to interpreter on startup.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph*
BASDIR
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Base filesystem directory as supplied (typically 
\family typewriter
./
\family default
).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph*
BREAKSST?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
By default,
 ^C will start a traceback.
 For debugging purposes,
 it can be useful to have it enter single step mode instead.
 If this name exists,
 it should be an integer flag:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\family typewriter
#1
\family default
 ⇒ single step on break
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\family typewriter
#0
\family default
 ⇒ trace back on break
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph*
DEDCONT?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Control the behavior of the interpreter's error handler.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\family typewriter
#1
\family default
 ⇒ continue execution on error
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\family typewriter
#0
\family default
 ⇒ trace back on error
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
All normal runtime errors,
 including ^C breaks,
 are suppressed if continuation is enabled.
 Errors can be checked with the 
\family typewriter
ISDED?

\family default
 flag,
 and parse errors will still be printed.
 Reasonable persons will leave this setting in its default state.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph*
EXCEPT
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This name must be present and must be code to successfully catch errors.
 The supplied 
\family typewriter
EXCEPT
\family default
 handles unwinding the call stack and preparing a list of contexts for the colorized traceback routine.
 It obeys all the flags mentioned in this section.
 To use an entirely custom error handler for a block of code,
 the name can be reused in a local variable context.
 It's wise to begin any such custom handler code with 
\family typewriter
'EXCEPT RM
\family default
,
 to uncover the system 
\family typewriter
EXCEPT
\family default
 and prevent your own error handler from recursing if it causes an error itself.
 It's wiser still to use 
\family typewriter
except
\family default
